      "Start with the view Aristotle broke from, and start with it fairly, because it was his own teacher's and he treated it with respect his whole life. Plato had argued that the changing, visible world your senses report on cannot be the source of real knowledge, because it never holds still (the Plato chapter). Real knowledge needs a stable object, so the really real must be something else: a separate, timeless realm of perfect, changeless **Forms** — the Beautiful itself, the Equal itself, Justice itself — of which the beautiful and equal and just things around you are only shifting, second-rate copies. Philosophy's job, on this view, is to climb out of the sensory world and grasp that higher realm through pure reason. The body is a distraction; the senses mislead; the truth lives above. This is not a strawman. It is a serious answer to a real problem, and it dominated the Academy where Aristotle spent half his life.",
