          "p": "Before the consequence, follow the potentiality-actuality pair up to its summit, because it leads to the strangest single thing in Aristotle's system — what he calls the **unmoved mover**, and what the whole structure of his metaphysics was quietly building toward (the argument lives in Book Lambda of the *Metaphysics*, the twelfth book of that compilation). Start from something obvious: things in the world *move* — they change, grow, fall, heat, rot. And every motion, Aristotle holds, is something being moved by something else: the cart by the horse, the horse by its hunger, on and on. But the chain cannot run backward forever; an infinite line of movers, each waiting on the one behind it, never gets the first push and so explains nothing. There has to be a first mover that starts the whole thing — and here is the twist that makes it *un*moved: it cannot itself be in motion, or it would just be one more link needing a mover behind it. So it must be pure **actuality**, with no unrealized potential left in it at all (recall the pair — potential is what *can still change*; a thing with zero potential cannot change, cannot move, is complete). Eternal, immaterial, perfect, finished."
