          "p": "And now the genuinely odd part, the part worth slowing down for. If the first mover never moves, how does it move *anything*? Aristotle's answer is beautiful and easy to miss: it moves things the way a goal moves you, or the way something beloved draws you across a room — *by being desired*, not by pushing. It is the final cause of all motion (Chapter 2), the perfection toward which everything else is reaching, the way the oak is what the acorn reaches toward. The whole cosmos is in motion because it is, so to speak, in love with the unmoved mover — drawn toward a completeness it imitates by moving. And what does this perfect being *do*, eternally? It thinks. But it cannot think about the changing world below it without depending on something lesser than itself, so it thinks about the only object worthy of it: itself. Aristotle's phrase is *noesis noeseos* — \"thought thinking itself,\" thinking about thinking — an eternal mind perfectly contemplating its own perfect activity. One last guardrail, the same one from Chapter 2: this is *not* the personal God of later religion. The unmoved mover did not create the world, does not know you exist, answers no prayers, and intervenes in nothing. It is the eternal terminus where explanation finally stops. Six centuries later Thomas Aquinas would seize this argument, fuse it with the God of the Bible, and make it the first of his famous proofs — but that re-pointing toward a caring creator was Aquinas's move, not Aristotle's."
