          "p": "And here is the sharp consequence, one of the most radical breaks in the whole corpus. If the soul is the *form* of the body, then it cannot pre-exist the body — there is no body for it to organize before there is a body — and it cannot survive the body's destruction, because the form of a thing that has been taken apart simply ceases to be the form of that thing, the way the shape of a melted statue is just *gone*. The soul does not float free; it does not transmigrate into a new body; it does not go on to remember the Forms in some afterlife. When the living thing dies, its soul ends with it. This is the flat opposite of Plato's immortal, traveling soul, and it follows inexorably from the hylomorphic move. (Aristotle does leave one small, much-debated opening: he hints that the *nous* — the highest intellect — might be in some way separable. Scholars argue about it endlessly, and it will preoccupy the great Arabic commentator Averroes centuries later. But the hylomorphic soul in general is mortal, and that was Aristotle's clearest break from his teacher.) The man who learned from Plato that the soul outlives the body concluded, after twenty years, that it doesn't."
